(* Session — the process constellation a battallion session runs as.
 *
 * A program-relative module of examples/battallion. `run` is the whole editor: it acquires the
 * terminal, spawns the kernel and the renderer, runs the supervisor's event loop, and restores
 * the terminal before returning an exit code. The caller supplies the buffer and the plugin set
 * (Editor.trp's header gives its shape); it keeps the root authority, which never leaves this
 * module's `run` frame.
 *
 *   supervisor  the process that calls `run`. Holds the root authority and the terminal: it
 *               subscribes to terminal events, traps SIGTERM, enters raw mode and the alternate
 *               screen, monitors the kernel, writes the buffer out when the kernel asks, and
 *               undoes all of that on the way out, whatever ended the session.
 *      kernel   the editor state machine. Receives forwarded events, folds the plugin set's
 *               keymap over the decoded keys, asks the renderer for a frame, and answers the
 *               supervisor.
 *      renderer holds the stdout descriptor and writes frames. Nothing else writes a frame.
 *
 * WHY THE SUPERVISOR IS THE SUBSCRIBER. The runtime's own safety net keys on the subscriber:
 * when an event arrives for a subscriber that has died, the runtime unsubscribes and restores
 * termios (rt/src/builtins/tty.mts:107-134). Making the process that must survive the session
 * the subscriber keeps that net behind the one process whose death would wedge the terminal.
 *
 * WHY EVERY EVENT IS ACKNOWLEDGED. The supervisor forwards one event and waits for the kernel's
 * answer before receiving the next, so a keystroke's frame is on the screen before the following
 * keystroke is read. The serialisation costs nothing at a human typing rate, and it is also the
 * frame in which the kernel can ask for something — see the write protocol below.
 *
 * A DEAD KERNEL IS FOUND BY `monitorlocal`; A WEDGED ONE BY A TIMER. The supervisor monitors the
 * kernel at spawn and reads the ("DONE", ref, pid, reason) that the runtime then delivers as a
 * lost kernel: it restores the terminal and exits `exitKernelLost`. The notification is matched
 * inside the same receives as everything else, needing no declassification and no helper process
 * — its presence level is the dying kernel's pc, which is bot, and every receive here runs at
 * [bot, channel level]; the measurement, for both an orderly and an error termination, is in
 * tests/_unautomated/claude/battallion-editor/probe-done-level.trp. What monitoring cannot see is
 * a kernel that is alive and no longer answering — one blocked on a renderer that died, say — so
 * every wait for the kernel still carries a watchdog timer (the `lib/timeout.trp` idiom, whose
 * message is matched inside the same ranged-receive region — probe-timer-level.trp), and a wait
 * the timer wins exits `exitKernelHung`. The two codes are kept apart because they say different
 * things about what went wrong.
 *
 * WHO WRITES THE FILE. The plugins are pure and the kernel holds a null-attenuated authority, while
 * `SimpleFileIO.writeFile` requires the root one — which never leaves the supervisor. So `:w`
 * does not write: it leaves a request in the state, the kernel sends the supervisor
 * ("WRITE", path, contents), the supervisor writes and answers ("WROTE", ok, message), which the
 * kernel folds back into the state before redrawing. The contents carry the channel level and the
 * write takes them unchanged — no confidentiality check stands between them and the file — and the
 * pc is unmoved by it, so the receive region still closes afterwards (probe-write-level.trp
 * measures all three).
 *
 * THE TARGET TRAVELS IN THE REQUEST, WHICH IS WHAT `:w PATH` NEEDS, and the authority does not
 * travel at all. A path the kernel asks for is a path the terminal could have named, and the bound
 * on it is `--io-root`: `SimpleFileIO.writeFile` resolves every path inside that subtree and
 * refuses one that leaves it, whether lexically or through a symlink, so the worst a keystroke can
 * do is name another file the session was already able to write. What stays with the supervisor is
 * the capability rather than the destination — the write happens in this frame, with the root
 * authority, and the kernel never holds one that could perform it.
 *
 * LABELS. Terminal events carry the channel level (ROOT by default), so everything the kernel
 * computes from a keystroke does too, and so does every frame and every byte written to the file.
 * Rather than declassify at the decoder, each of the three processes receives at the interval
 * [bot, channel level]: the supervisor in a certified region opened with the root authority, the
 * kernel and the renderer in confined regions opened with a null-attenuated authority, which they
 * never close because they receive for their whole lives (lib/Tty.trp's `listen`, and
 * probe-render-path.trp). The kernel therefore runs permanently tainted by the terminal, which is
 * what it is: a process whose entire state is what the terminal said. No value is declassified
 * anywhere in battallion.
 *
 * HANDLERS PROJECT; EFFECTS HAPPEN AFTER THE RECEIVE RETURNS. An effect inside an `hn` body is
 * refused while a ranged-receive region is open, with a diagnostic about a branch that the
 * program does not contain (probe-render-path.trp records the exact failure). Every receive
 * below returns a value and acts on it afterwards.
 *
 * THE REGION IS OPENED AND CLOSED IN STRAIGHT-LINE CODE, per lib/Tty.trp: `Tty.listen` sits in
 * `run`'s startup sequence and `Tty.unlisten` in its teardown sequence, with the whole event
 * loop between them. That extends to `run`'s caller: a region can only be closed from its floor,
 * and code inside a branch on a channel-level value runs above it, so `Session.run` must itself
 * be called from straight-line code. bt.trp's header says how, and
 * tests/_unautomated/claude/battallion-viewer/probe-pc-and-unlisten.trp measures it.
 *)

import IfcUtil
import Outcome
import SimpleFileIO
import Tty
import timeout
import "./Screen"
import "./Editor"
import "./Key"

let (** How long the supervisor waits for the kernel before calling it wedged. Two seconds is far
     *  above any frame the editor draws — a megabyte buffer's line read is measured in
     *  microseconds (_dev_planning/text-editor/rope-benchmarks.md) — and far below a person's
     *  patience with a wedged terminal. *)
    val watchdogMs = 2000

    (** How long a pending escape sequence waits before the kernel reads it as final. A lone ESC
     *  is either the Esc key or the first byte of a sequence and only elapsed time separates them
     *  (Key.trp), so pressing Esc costs this much latency and nothing else does. Fifty
     *  milliseconds is the range terminal emulators and their clients have long settled on: long
     *  enough that a sequence arriving in pieces over a slow link is not torn apart, short enough
     *  that leaving insert mode feels immediate. *)
    val escapeMs = 50

    (** The session ended the way it was asked to: `q`, `:q`, CTRL-c, end of input, or SIGTERM. *)
    val exitOk = 0

    (** The kernel is gone: it crashed, or it terminated when it had no business terminating. *)
    val exitKernelLost = 3

    (** The kernel is alive and stopped answering. *)
    val exitKernelHung = 4

    (** The terminal size to assume when stdout is not a terminal, so that a session started over
     *  a pipe still runs rather than failing at startup. *)
    val fallbackCols = 80
    val fallbackRows = 24

    (*=============================  The renderer process  =============================*)

    (** Write frames until told to stop. The frame carries the kernel's pid, which is answered
     *  with "RENDERED" so that the kernel knows the screen is up to date before it releases the
     *  supervisor. *)
    fun rendererLoop (out, lev) =
        let val m = rcv ( IfcUtil.bot, lev
                        , [ hn ("FRAME", from, lines, width, curRow, curCol, tail) =>
                                ("FRAME", from, lines, width, curRow, curCol, tail)
                          , hn "STOP" => "STOP" ])
        in case m of
                ("FRAME", from, lines, width, curRow, curCol, tail) =>
                    let val _ = fwrite (out, Screen.frame (lines, width, curRow, curCol, tail))
                        val _ = send (from, "RENDERED")
                    in rendererLoop (out, lev)
                    end
              | _ => ()
        end

    (** The renderer's whole life. The loop is called with its result bound rather than in tail
     *  position: the region opened above is never closed, and a frame that opened one must not
     *  end in a tail call — the loop's eventual return would then unwind past this frame into a
     *  caller's, where the mailbox-clearance balance check refuses the mismatch. The measurement
     *  is in tests/_unautomated/claude/battallion-viewer/probe-region-tail-call.trp. *)
    fun renderer (out, lev, weak) =
        let val (ok, cap) = enableRangedReceive (IfcUtil.bot, lev, weak)
            val _ = rendererLoop (out, lev)
        in ()
        end

    (*==============================  The kernel process  ==============================*)

    (** Hand the renderer a frame of `st` and wait until it has been written. The frame carries the
     *  cursor-shape escape the plugin set picked for this state; the renderer writes it after the
     *  cursor is in place. *)
    fun draw (rend, lev, plugins, st) =
        let val (lines, curRow, curCol, tail) = Editor.frameOf (plugins, st)
            val _ = send (rend, ("FRAME", self (), lines, st.cols, curRow, curCol, tail))
            val _ = rcv (IfcUtil.bot, lev, [ hn "RENDERED" => () ])
        in ()
        end

    (** The keymap folded over a list of keys. Stops at the first key that quits and at the first
     *  key that asks the kernel for something, handing back the keys it has not reached: a `:w`
     *  in the middle of a chunk must be carried out before the keys after it are folded, or the
     *  file would be written from a state the user has already typed past.
     *
     *  This is the one call site a delivered plugin closure would be folded at, and the one that
     *  would then wrap the call in `guard` — see Editor.trp's header for what that buys and what
     *  it costs. *)
    fun applyKeys (plugins, st, [])        = (st, false, [])
      | applyKeys (plugins, st, k :: rest) =
          let val (st', quit) = plugins.onKey (st, k)
          in if quit then (st', true, rest)
             else if Editor.hasRequest st' then (st', false, rest)
             else applyKeys (plugins, st', rest)
          end

    (** Fold the keys, carrying out any request they raise. A write is a round trip through the
     *  supervisor — the authority is there, not here — and the outcome goes back into the state
     *  before the remaining keys are folded. *)
    fun runKeys (sup, lev, plugins, st, keys) =
        let val (st1, quit, rest) = applyKeys (plugins, st, keys)
        in if Editor.hasRequest st1
           then let val _ = send (sup, ("WRITE", Editor.writePath st1, Editor.contents st1))
                    val (ok, message) = rcv ( IfcUtil.bot, lev
                                            , [ hn ("WROTE", o, m) => (o, m) ])
                    val (st2, quitAfter) = plugins.onWritten (st1, ok, message)
                in if quit orelse quitAfter then (st2, true)
                   else runKeys (sup, lev, plugins, st2, rest)
                end
           else (st1, quit)
        end

    (** Arm the escape timer and return the nonce that identifies it. Timers are never cancelled
     *  — a spent one is told from a live one by its nonce, and consumed and dropped either way. *)
    fun armEscape () =
        let val nonce = mkuuid ()
            val _ = timeout.timeout (self ()) ("ESCWAIT", nonce) escapeMs
        in nonce
        end

    (** One message: decode, fold, redraw, answer. `carry` is the decoder's remainder, the bytes
     *  of an escape sequence that has not finished arriving, and `nonce` identifies the timer
     *  armed for it.
     *
     *  A message is one of three things. A forwarded terminal event is folded and answered, so
     *  that the supervisor takes the next event only once this one's frame is on the screen. An
     *  escape timer that is still current means the carry will not grow: `Key.flush` reads it as
     *  final — a lone ESC becomes the Esc key — and the result is drawn but *not* answered,
     *  because no forwarded event is outstanding and an answer would release a supervisor that is
     *  not waiting. Anything else — a timer whose nonce has been superseded — is dropped.
     *
     *  A timer is armed only where the carry was set, i.e. on a forwarded event, and never on the
     *  strength of a carry that is merely still there. Re-arming on a superseded timer would give
     *  a carry that never completes two outstanding timers that supersede each other in turn: each
     *  would arrive stale, arm its successor, and invalidate the one due to fire, so the flush
     *  that clears the carry would never come and the kernel would spin for the rest of the
     *  session. Arming at the one place the carry changes leaves exactly one live timer. *)
    fun kernelLoop (sup, rend, lev, plugins, st, carry, nonce) =
        let val m = rcv ( IfcUtil.bot, lev
                        , [ hn ("KEY", bytes)        => ("KEY", bytes)
                          , hn ("RESIZE", cols, rows) => ("RESIZE", cols, rows)
                          , hn ("ESCWAIT", n)         => ("ESCWAIT", n) ])
            val (st0, keys, carry0, answer, act, rearm) =
                    case m of
                         ("KEY", bytes) =>
                             let val (ks, rest) = Key.decode (carry ^ bytes)
                             in (st, ks, rest, true, true, rest <> "")
                             end
                       | ("RESIZE", cols, rows) =>
                             (Editor.onResize (st, cols, rows), [], carry, true, true, false)
                       | ("ESCWAIT", n) =>
                             if n = nonce andalso carry <> ""
                             then (st, Key.flush carry, "", false, true, false)
                             else (st, [], carry, false, false, false)
                       | _ => (st, [], carry, false, false, false)
            val (st', quit) = runKeys (sup, lev, plugins, st0, keys)
            val nonce' = if rearm then armEscape () else nonce
            val _ = if quit
                    then send (sup, "QUIT")
                    else if act
                    then let val _ = draw (rend, lev, plugins, st')
                         in if answer then send (sup, "ACK") else ()
                         end
                    else ()
        in if quit then () else kernelLoop (sup, rend, lev, plugins, st', carry0, nonce')
        end

    (** Draw the first frame, tell the supervisor the session is on the screen, then loop. The
     *  loop's result is bound rather than tail-called, for the reason given at `renderer`. *)
    fun kernel (sup, rend, lev, weak, plugins, st) =
        let val (ok, cap) = enableRangedReceive (IfcUtil.bot, lev, weak)
            val _ = draw (rend, lev, plugins, st)
            val _ = send (sup, "READY")
            val _ = kernelLoop (sup, rend, lev, plugins, st, "", mkuuid ())
        in ()
        end

    (*============================  The supervisor process  ============================*)

    (** Wait for the kernel's next word, under the watchdog and the monitor. Returns a tagged
     *  triple: ("ready" | "ack" | "quit" | "sigterm" | "hung" | "dead", "", "") or
     *  ("write", path, contents).
     *
     *  SIGTERM is answered here as well as in the event loop so that a signal arriving while a
     *  frame is being drawn is acted on at once rather than after the frame.
     *
     *  A kernel that quits sends "QUIT" and only then terminates, so its own ("DONE", …) is
     *  behind "QUIT" in the mailbox and a receive that scans in arrival order takes "QUIT" first
     *  (rt/src/MailboxProcessor.mts:100-131). The notification is left where it lies. *)
    fun awaitKernel (me, lev, kref) =
        let val nonce = mkuuid ()
            val _ = timeout.timeout me ("WATCHDOG", nonce) watchdogMs
        in rcv ( IfcUtil.bot, lev
               , [ hn "READY"                => ("ready", "", "")
                 , hn "ACK"                  => ("ack", "", "")
                 , hn "QUIT"                 => ("quit", "", "")
                 , hn ("WRITE", path, text)  => ("write", path, text)
                 , hn "SIGTERM"              => ("sigterm", "", "")
                 , hn ("DONE", r, p, reason) when r = kref => ("dead", "", "")
                 , hn ("WATCHDOG", n) when n = nonce       => ("hung", "", "") ])
        end

    (** Write the buffer to the file the request named. `(true, "written")`, or `(false, reason)`
     *  for a write the runtime refused — a path outside `--io-root`, a read-only file — which the
     *  status line reports and the session survives. *)
    fun writeBuffer (auth, path, text) =
        case SimpleFileIO.writeFile (auth, path, text) of
             OK _  => (true, "written")
           | ERR e => (false, e.reason)

    (** Wait for the kernel's answer, carrying out the writes it asks for on the way. Returns the
     *  first answer that is not a request. *)
    fun serve (auth, me, kern, lev, kref) =
        let val (tag, path, text) = awaitKernel (me, lev, kref)
        in if tag = "write"
           then let val (ok, message) = writeBuffer (auth, path, text)
                    val _ = send (kern, ("WROTE", ok, message))
                in serve (auth, me, kern, lev, kref)
                end
           else tag
        end

    (** The event loop. One terminal event at a time is forwarded to the kernel and answered
     *  before the next is taken.
     *
     *  The extra handlers pick up SIGTERM, the kernel's death while no answer is outstanding, and
     *  the spent watchdog timers of events the kernel answered in time: a timer whose nonce no
     *  `awaitKernel` is waiting for any more would otherwise sit in the mailbox for the rest of
     *  the session. *)
    fun eventLoop (auth, me, kern, lev, kref) =
        let val ev = Tty.nextEventWith ( lev
                                       , [ hn "SIGTERM"       => "sigterm"
                                         , hn ("WATCHDOG", _) => "stale"
                                         , hn ("DONE", r, p, reason) => "dead" ])
            val (forward, msg) =
                    case ev of TTY_DATA b               => (true, ("KEY", b))
                             | TTY_RESIZE {cols, rows}  => (true, ("RESIZE", cols, rows))
                             | TTY_EOF                  => (false, "quit")
                             | other                    => (false, other)
            val answer = if forward
                         then let val _ = send (kern, msg)
                              in serve (auth, me, kern, lev, kref)
                              end
                         else if msg = "sigterm" then "quit" else msg
        in      if answer = "ack"   then eventLoop (auth, me, kern, lev, kref)
           else if answer = "stale" then eventLoop (auth, me, kern, lev, kref)
           else if answer = "dead"  then exitKernelLost
           else if answer = "hung"  then exitKernelHung
           else exitOk
        end

    (** The terminal's size, or the fallback when stdout is not a terminal. *)
    fun terminalSize out =
        case Tty.size out of OK s  => (s.cols, s.rows)
                           | ERR _ => (fallbackCols, fallbackRows)

    (** The session's exit code, given the kernel's first word. Named so that `run`'s own body
     *  stays a flat sequence with the region's open and close in it and nothing else between
     *  them but this one call. *)
    fun sessionCode (auth, me, kern, lev, kref, first) =
             if first = "ready" then eventLoop (auth, me, kern, lev, kref)
        else if first = "dead"  then exitKernelLost
        else if first = "hung"  then exitKernelHung
        else exitOk

    (** Run a session over `buffer`, with `plugins` as the plugin set, and return an exit code.
     *
     *  `auth` must be the root authority: subscribing, raw mode, the SIGTERM trap, the certified
     *  receive region and the file write all need it. It is not passed to the kernel or the
     *  renderer, which get a null-attenuated authority sufficient only to open a confined region,
     *  and it is not passed to a plugin at all — a plugin's whole reach is the state it is handed
     *  back. *)
    fun run (auth, buffer, path, plugins) =
        let val i = stdin auth
            val out = stdout auth
            val weak = attenuate (auth, IfcUtil.null)
            val me = self ()
            val lev = Tty.level i

            val (cols, rows) = terminalSize out

            (*--- Acquire the terminal. ---*)
            val _ = trapSigterm (auth, me)
            val _ = Tty.subscribe (i, me)
            val _ = Tty.rawMode (i, true)
            val _ = fwrite (out, Screen.enterAlt)

            (*--- The constellation. `demonitorlocal` is a stub in this runtime, so the kernel is
                  monitored exactly once and the reference is never released. ---*)
            val rend = spawn (fn () => renderer (out, lev, weak))
            val st0  = Editor.init (buffer, path, cols, rows)
            val kern = spawn (fn () => kernel (me, rend, lev, weak, plugins, st0))
            val kref = monitorlocal kern

            (*--- The region, opened and closed in straight-line code. The certification bit
                  decides the close: at a channel level the certification refuses — a
                  null-integrity ceiling under NMIFC refuses even the root authority — the
                  enable still succeeds but yields a confined region with no close, and an
                  unconditional unlisten would be a thread error HERE, before every line that
                  gives the terminal back (the downgrading analysis's finding F1,
                  _dev_planning/text-editor/downgrading-principles.md). A confined region is
                  merely left open: the session runs identically inside it, and the process
                  exits by `exit`, never by returning through this frame. The close cannot sit
                  inside a branch (the clearance record must match the branch-entry snapshot),
                  so the branch selects a closure and the call is straight-line — the shape
                  probe-conditional-close*.trp pins. ---*)
            val (ok, cap) = Tty.listen (i, auth)
            val unlisten = if ok then (fn () => Tty.unlisten cap) else (fn () => ())
            val (first, _, _) = awaitKernel (me, lev, kref)
            val code = sessionCode (auth, me, kern, lev, kref, first)
            val _ = unlisten ()

            (*--- Give the terminal back. Cooked mode first: it is the state a terminal is
                  least usable in, so it is the one to leave earliest, and it makes the last
                  escapes written the observable proof that the mode is already back. The show is
                  idempotent — a frame leaves the cursor shown and only a session cut short between
                  a frame's hide and its show leaves it hidden — and it is written unconditionally
                  because the state of a session ending on a dead kernel is not knowable here. The
                  cursor's appearance is the plugin set's to undo, and it is undone here rather
                  than by the kernel because the kernel may be the reason the session is ending. ---*)
            val _ = Tty.rawMode (i, false)
            val _ = fwrite (out, Screen.showCursor ^ plugins.cursorReset ^ Screen.leaveAlt)
            val _ = Tty.unsubscribe i
            val _ = untrapSigterm auth
            val _ = send (rend, "STOP")
        in code
        end

in [ ("watchdogMs", watchdogMs)
   , ("escapeMs", escapeMs)
   , ("exitOk", exitOk)
   , ("exitKernelLost", exitKernelLost)
   , ("exitKernelHung", exitKernelHung)
   , ("run", run)
   ]
end
